1
00:00:06,047 --> 00:00:07,924
[engine revving]

2
00:00:08,007 --> 00:00:09,676
["Floss Like A Boss" plays]

3
00:00:15,598 --> 00:00:20,228
[Jerry] This is a 2018 Lamborghini
Huracán Performante.

4
00:00:20,311 --> 00:00:22,564
We don't do a lot of new cars
on the show.

5
00:00:22,647 --> 00:00:24,649
We feel that new cars lack personality.

6
00:00:24,983 --> 00:00:27,193
This car does not lack personality.

7
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:29,404
With 631 horsepower

8
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:31,781
from a 5.2 liter V10,

9
00:00:32,031 --> 00:00:34,909
the Huracán does zero to 60
in 2.9 seconds.

10
00:00:34,993 --> 00:00:37,287
-[revving]
-Do you have that kind of time?

11
00:00:37,370 --> 00:00:40,790
Lamborghini likes to name their cars
after famous fighting bulls.

12
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:44,085
The Huracán was a bull
that fought in the 1870s.

13
00:00:44,169 --> 00:00:46,546
Imagine if that bull could see this car.

14
00:00:46,629 --> 00:00:49,674
I don't think it would react.
I have never seen a bull react.

15
00:00:50,216 --> 00:00:51,301
To anything.

16
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,387
They just keep staring,
like people do with this car.

17
00:00:54,804 --> 00:00:57,891
A Lamborghini is for a certain kind
of person that wants to make an entrance,

18
00:00:57,974 --> 00:00:59,309
who wants to make a statement,

19
00:00:59,392 --> 00:01:01,978
does not want to slip by unnoticed.

20
00:01:02,145 --> 00:01:04,022
This is a car for people
whose attitude is...

21
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:05,732
I'm not gonna be ignored!

22
00:01:05,815 --> 00:01:09,527
[Jerry] What I like about Lamborghinis
is they don't care too much about reality.

23
00:01:09,611 --> 00:01:12,530
They're fantasy cars for people
who are living in fantasy worlds.

24
00:01:12,614 --> 00:01:16,117
So today, I'm gonna act out a fantasy
with a very close friend of mine,

25
00:01:16,201 --> 00:01:20,497
the incredibly funny, talented,
irresistible Matthew Broderick

26
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:22,165
Where are we, Matthew?

27
00:01:22,248 --> 00:01:25,543
Matthew and I love to talk 
about classic comedy and baseball.

28
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:28,588
That is our relationship,
and I'll bet you we do it again today.

29
00:01:28,671 --> 00:01:30,924
We really got lucky with the weather.

30
00:01:31,007 --> 00:01:34,302
Yes, we would've had to just talk
in a nice, comfortable coffee shop.

31
00:01:34,385 --> 00:01:36,554
[Jerry] Which is why I am Jerry Seinfeld,

32
00:01:36,638 --> 00:01:39,808
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

33
00:01:44,187 --> 00:01:45,605
-[Matthew] Hello?
-[Jerry] Matthew!

34
00:01:46,106 --> 00:01:48,817
-[Matthew] How are you?
-[Jerry] I'm so excited.

35
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,902
-[Matthew] Me too. Me too.
-[Jerry] Really?

36
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:53,029
-[Matthew] Yeah.
-[Jerry] We just get to be together

37
00:01:53,113 --> 00:01:54,989
and that's just always fun.

38
00:01:55,073 --> 00:01:56,699
[Matthew] Yeah. I'm stoked.

39
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:58,868
[Jerry] Oh, that's a great word...

40
00:01:58,952 --> 00:02:00,995
-[Matthew laughs]
-[Jerry] ...if you're an idiot!

41
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:03,248
I'm pulling up to your house right now,

42
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:04,958
-and we're gonna start off...
-Okay.

43
00:02:05,041 --> 00:02:07,001
...with a fantasy.
Be there in a couple of minutes.

44
00:02:07,085 --> 00:02:08,127
[Matthew] I'm very curious.

45
00:02:08,878 --> 00:02:09,712
[doorbell rings]

46
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:11,881
-[Jerry] Hi, Matthew.
-Hello.

47
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:13,716
-Good to see you.
-Good to see you, too.

48
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,427
Okay, my idea for this day...

49
00:02:17,595 --> 00:02:18,429
Yeah.

50
00:02:19,264 --> 00:02:20,849
...is you and I

51
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:24,602
have had these careers
in the entertainment industry,

52
00:02:24,686 --> 00:02:27,188
which was our dream.
That's what we wanted to do.

53
00:02:27,272 --> 00:02:29,274
-We wanted to be entertainers.
-We wanted to be that.

54
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:31,192
-Yeah.
-And then we got to do that.

55
00:02:31,276 --> 00:02:32,986
-It really worked out well.
-Yeah.

56
00:02:33,945 --> 00:02:35,822
But we had another dream.

57
00:02:35,905 --> 00:02:37,156
And that dream...

58
00:02:38,449 --> 00:02:40,243
was to be baseball players.

59
00:02:40,326 --> 00:02:41,703
-[Matthew] Uh-huh.
-[Jerry laughs]

60
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:45,248
[Jerry] Which did not work out.
But if we did become baseball players,

61
00:02:45,331 --> 00:02:47,542
this is the kind of car we would buy.

62
00:02:47,625 --> 00:02:48,877
[laughing] Right.

63
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,337
Young baseball players
drive cars like this.

64
00:02:51,421 --> 00:02:53,548
-Yeah.
-They want to get attention.

65
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:54,924
-Of course.
-They want the world--

66
00:02:55,008 --> 00:02:57,218
-Their dream has come true.
-"Yeah. I made it!

67
00:02:57,302 --> 00:02:58,720
I made it to the bigs."

68
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:00,513
[revs]

69
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:02,765
-Wow.
-Yeah.

70
00:03:02,849 --> 00:03:04,350
It also has this mode.

71
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:05,643
[revving]

72
00:03:05,727 --> 00:03:09,063
Irritate the neighbor mo--
What is that mode called?

73
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:10,565
Neighbor Irritablé.

74
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:18,281
And what's your coffee, um, routine?

75
00:03:18,907 --> 00:03:22,869
Like, two double espressos
in the morning.

76
00:03:23,244 --> 00:03:24,078
Wow.

77
00:03:24,162 --> 00:03:25,371
Do you make them yourself?

78
00:03:25,455 --> 00:03:26,497
I make them myself.

79
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:28,208
But you push one button,

80
00:03:28,291 --> 00:03:29,375
you put a pod in.

81
00:03:29,459 --> 00:03:31,711
-Oh. Oh, that's awful.
-So, not really "making."

82
00:03:32,295 --> 00:03:33,254
Thank you.

83
00:03:33,338 --> 00:03:34,631
[Jerry laughs]

84
00:03:34,714 --> 00:03:36,007
[jazz plays]

85
00:03:36,299 --> 00:03:38,551
-Pretty good coffee, though.
-I know, it is. You're right.

86
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:42,055
-I've had it and it is pretty good.
-I know, but it takes all the, uh...

87
00:03:42,138 --> 00:03:44,515
the connection to the bean.

88
00:03:44,974 --> 00:03:46,351
-The connection to the bean.
-Yeah.

89
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:47,268
-Yes.
-I like that.

90
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:48,811
-I miss that.
-I like that.

91
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:53,858
-[Jerry] Village Cigars is still here.
-Still here since I was a little kid,

92
00:03:54,108 --> 00:03:56,152
where I grew up three blocks from here.

93
00:03:56,611 --> 00:04:01,157
What is it like to have spent
your entire life in one place?

94
00:04:01,241 --> 00:04:03,451
-You've been here your entire life.
-It's really strange.

95
00:04:03,534 --> 00:04:06,704
-Yeah, my first play was there even.
-Yeah.

96
00:04:06,788 --> 00:04:08,248
-Uh...
-You're like Jesus.

97
00:04:08,331 --> 00:04:10,583
I am a little like Jesus. [laughing]

98
00:04:10,667 --> 00:04:13,336
Sometimes I think it's really cool
that I, um...

99
00:04:13,419 --> 00:04:15,171
that I've always been in the same place,

100
00:04:15,255 --> 00:04:18,007
and sometimes I think,
"Well, it's... it's tragic actually."

101
00:04:18,091 --> 00:04:21,135
I just never, uh... I never saw anything.
Nothing happened.

102
00:04:21,469 --> 00:04:23,638
The name of the book
is Nothing Happened.

103
00:04:23,721 --> 00:04:25,014
-Yeah.
-[Jerry laughing]

104
00:04:25,598 --> 00:04:27,225
This is the building I grew up in.

105
00:04:27,308 --> 00:04:29,227
This gray one right here.

106
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:30,937
I used to swim back there.

107
00:04:31,187 --> 00:04:33,648
That's the, uh, Leroy Street pool.

108
00:04:33,982 --> 00:04:34,816
[both laugh]

109
00:04:34,899 --> 00:04:37,485
At night...
There was a big hole in the fence,

110
00:04:37,610 --> 00:04:41,823
and neighborhood youth
would go through it and swim at night

111
00:04:41,906 --> 00:04:43,992
with no adults or anything.

112
00:04:44,075 --> 00:04:45,410
Did you feel very, uh,

113
00:04:45,493 --> 00:04:47,662
-Bowery Boys when you did that?
-I did.

114
00:04:47,870 --> 00:04:49,163
I felt like a Dead End Kid.

115
00:04:49,247 --> 00:04:50,164
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

116
00:04:50,248 --> 00:04:52,083
"Ah, gee," you know?

117
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:56,504
All right, so...

118
00:04:57,005 --> 00:04:57,964
So...

119
00:04:58,047 --> 00:05:00,842
Oh, I thought... I thought you were
ejecting me for a second.

120
00:05:00,925 --> 00:05:02,593
[Jerry laughs]

121
00:05:02,677 --> 00:05:05,138
Doesn't it look like one of those?
You flip it up, boom!

122
00:05:05,221 --> 00:05:07,765
That's so that you don't
accidentally eject anybody.

123
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:10,184
-[Jerry] It's a little chilly.
-[Matthew] It's a lot chilly.

124
00:05:10,268 --> 00:05:11,936
I thought maybe we should get some

125
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:13,646
-things to stay warm.
-[Matthew] Yeah.

126
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:16,149
-[man] I'm sorry, guys. I apologize.
-[Matthew] We can't go in.

127
00:05:16,232 --> 00:05:18,484
[man] I might lose my job
if you guys coming in here, so...

128
00:05:18,568 --> 00:05:20,320
-Oh, so we're not coming in?
-Oh.

129
00:05:20,403 --> 00:05:21,237
[Jerry] Oh, okay. 

130
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:22,697
-[man] Sorry about that.
-All right.

131
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:24,574
-[man] Big fan of the show....
-Oh.

132
00:05:24,657 --> 00:05:26,868
-Gee, you were almost on it.
-[all laugh]

133
00:05:27,410 --> 00:05:29,454
-Is that a first for your show?
-Yeah, that's a first.

134
00:05:29,537 --> 00:05:32,081
It had something to do with me,
do you think? Or...

135
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:35,626
How about this one
with all the mismatched--

136
00:05:35,710 --> 00:05:36,794
[Matthew] The Jenga.

137
00:05:36,878 --> 00:05:38,713
[Jerry] Oh, the Jenga. Yeah, yeah.

138
00:05:38,796 --> 00:05:40,798
Like they needed to do one more drawing.

139
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:42,967
-[Jerry laughs]
-"Oh, I'll get to it later."

140
00:05:43,051 --> 00:05:45,011
"Well, we already... we built it already."

141
00:05:45,094 --> 00:05:46,637
Okay. Well...

142
00:05:47,638 --> 00:05:48,473
I had a bit.

143
00:05:48,556 --> 00:05:50,641
"You don't just talk in marriage.

144
00:05:50,975 --> 00:05:52,685
-It's Jenga.
-[both laugh]

145
00:05:52,769 --> 00:05:55,730
You have to wiggle that block a little
before you slide it."

146
00:05:56,064 --> 00:05:56,898
[Matthew laughs]

147
00:05:56,981 --> 00:06:00,651
Can I... Is this something I could say,
or should I not say it?

148
00:06:00,985 --> 00:06:02,028
[jazz plays]

149
00:06:05,114 --> 00:06:06,074
-[woman] Hi.
-[both] Hi.

150
00:06:06,157 --> 00:06:07,700
-[woman] How are you?
-Good. How are you?

151
00:06:07,784 --> 00:06:10,161
-[woman] Good.
-[stammering] Uh, can we get some coffees?

152
00:06:10,244 --> 00:06:11,079
[woman] Sure.

153
00:06:11,829 --> 00:06:14,290
I, of course, didn't bring any...
any money.

154
00:06:14,707 --> 00:06:15,958
I didn't either.

155
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:17,752
I have some in the car,
in the Lamborghini.

156
00:06:17,835 --> 00:06:19,128
[woman] Cafe latte?

157
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:22,173
-That's so obnoxious.
-[laughing] It's awful.

158
00:06:22,256 --> 00:06:24,801
"I left it in my Lamborghini.
Perhaps I could, uh...

159
00:06:24,884 --> 00:06:26,135
You'd trust me for this?"

160
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:29,180
-Obviously. I have a Lamborghini, so...
-[Matthew laughs]

161
00:06:29,263 --> 00:06:31,432
-I'm certainly good for it."
-[both laugh]

162
00:06:31,516 --> 00:06:34,811
-Can I get you anything else?
-[Jerry] Maybe this, uh, pain au chocolat.

163
00:06:34,894 --> 00:06:36,521
-Pain au chocolat?
-Please.

164
00:06:36,854 --> 00:06:38,815
-Two of those.
-Oh, boy.

165
00:06:38,898 --> 00:06:42,235
Maybe just put it on a little, uh...
Or is that the way you normally do it?

166
00:06:42,318 --> 00:06:43,945
-Normally, I...
-Okay, that's fine.

167
00:06:44,028 --> 00:06:47,031
I don't want to disrupt anything.
We had enough trouble at Patagonia.

168
00:06:47,115 --> 00:06:48,616
[Matthew laughs]

169
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:50,118
[upbeat jazz plays]

170
00:06:51,786 --> 00:06:53,913
[Jerry] I love restaurant bits.

171
00:06:53,996 --> 00:06:56,874
I watch a lot of YouTube.
WC Fields sketch popped up,

172
00:06:56,958 --> 00:06:58,751
and he asks, "Can I get some service?"

173
00:06:58,835 --> 00:07:00,461
So, the menu would be out,

174
00:07:00,545 --> 00:07:02,338
and he would say, "I'll have the eggs,"

175
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:04,507
and she would take out a pencil and go...

176
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:06,717
-"We don't have that."
-[both laugh]

177
00:07:07,677 --> 00:07:12,431
And each thing he... he asked for,
she would take the pencil out again...

178
00:07:12,515 --> 00:07:13,433
-Yeah.
-And...

179
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:15,184
-I love it.
-[Jerry laughs]

180
00:07:15,435 --> 00:07:16,644
And the timing.

181
00:07:16,727 --> 00:07:19,605
They probably did it around the country,
you know, like...

182
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:22,316
-Yeah. That's the good stuff, right?
-Yeah.

183
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:23,943
[jazz plays]

184
00:07:27,947 --> 00:07:28,948
This stinks.

185
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:30,908
[Jerry laughs]

186
00:07:30,992 --> 00:07:32,743
Crazy to say something like that?

187
00:07:33,077 --> 00:07:34,370
[both laugh]

188
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:36,122
Look what happened in Patagonia.

189
00:07:37,373 --> 00:07:38,499
"I could lose my job."

190
00:07:38,916 --> 00:07:40,751
The man almost lost his job.

191
00:07:40,835 --> 00:07:41,961
[both laugh]

192
00:07:43,212 --> 00:07:46,090
But he was really pained, wasn't he?

193
00:07:46,174 --> 00:07:49,135
He looked like...
like one of those World War II movies

194
00:07:49,218 --> 00:07:51,846
where you don't know
that the Nazis are in the other room.

195
00:07:51,929 --> 00:07:53,931
"No, no, we're closed."
You know, like,

196
00:07:54,015 --> 00:07:56,476
"Come back tomorrow.
That would be a much better idea."

197
00:07:56,559 --> 00:07:57,560
[Jerry laughs]

198
00:07:58,186 --> 00:08:01,606
You sure everything's all right here?
"Everything's just fine, yeah."

199
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:04,150
Let me tell you what's gonna happen.

200
00:08:04,775 --> 00:08:05,985
We're going back there.

201
00:08:06,068 --> 00:08:07,111
[Matthew laughs]

202
00:08:07,528 --> 00:08:08,821
-Back to Patagonia?
-[Jerry] Yeah.

203
00:08:09,989 --> 00:08:12,658
-[Matthew] I think this is pretty good.
-It stinks.

204
00:08:14,410 --> 00:08:15,578
All right, on we go.

205
00:08:16,829 --> 00:08:19,207
[Jerry] I don't like to walk
with a coffee. You?

206
00:08:20,041 --> 00:08:21,083
Not really.

207
00:08:21,167 --> 00:08:22,668
[Jerry] I don't like the look.

208
00:08:22,752 --> 00:08:25,796
When you see people in LA,
pictures or...

209
00:08:26,756 --> 00:08:28,466
-you know, paparazzi pictures...
-Yeah.

210
00:08:28,549 --> 00:08:31,010
-...they always have a water, a liquid.
-Always!

211
00:08:31,093 --> 00:08:33,387
-Always a liquid.
-Why did you need to be drinking

212
00:08:33,471 --> 00:08:35,014
-all the time?
-Yes.

213
00:08:35,097 --> 00:08:37,099
The guy on TMZ,

214
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:38,935
while he's eviscerating people,

215
00:08:39,018 --> 00:08:41,687
-has that little baby bottle with him.
-Always. Yep.

216
00:08:41,771 --> 00:08:43,231
-Yeah.
-[Jerry laughs]

217
00:08:44,982 --> 00:08:46,108
Let's try this again.

218
00:08:46,984 --> 00:08:47,818
[Jerry] Hi.

219
00:08:47,902 --> 00:08:49,403
-[woman] How are you?
-[Matthew] Hello.

220
00:08:49,487 --> 00:08:51,239
-Are we okay now?
-[woman] Yes.

221
00:08:51,322 --> 00:08:52,240
Okay.

222
00:08:52,323 --> 00:08:53,991
[upbeat music plays]

223
00:09:01,415 --> 00:09:02,875
That other guy's okay, right?

224
00:09:03,501 --> 00:09:04,502
Tell him we're sorry.

225
00:09:05,044 --> 00:09:06,671
That guy's in the back tied up.

226
00:09:06,754 --> 00:09:08,214
[Jerry laughs]

227
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:12,635
I really want to watch
that diner scene with you now.

228
00:09:12,718 --> 00:09:14,720
[WC Fields] Ah, good morning, beautiful!

229
00:09:15,638 --> 00:09:16,847
Got a menu?

230
00:09:18,307 --> 00:09:19,892
[both laugh]

231
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:22,645
Thank you. Thank you.

232
00:09:23,646 --> 00:09:25,564
Is that steak New York cut?

233
00:09:26,107 --> 00:09:27,066
[Jerry laughs]

234
00:09:30,236 --> 00:09:31,279
[both laugh]

235
00:09:31,862 --> 00:09:34,574
I mean, that's how you say a set-up line.

236
00:09:35,616 --> 00:09:37,076
-Right?
-Yeah.

237
00:09:37,159 --> 00:09:38,452
Loud and clear.

238
00:09:38,536 --> 00:09:39,453
[Jerry laughs]

239
00:09:40,830 --> 00:09:42,915
[laughs] So much water.

240
00:09:44,458 --> 00:09:48,754
And there's absolutely no way the cubes
could actually have gotten in his pants.

241
00:09:49,463 --> 00:09:51,132
-That's excellent.
-Yeah.

242
00:09:51,716 --> 00:09:54,093
Nothing funnier to me
than something like...

243
00:09:54,719 --> 00:09:57,430
very basic kind of setups like that.

244
00:09:57,513 --> 00:09:58,806
Like the Patagonia guy.

245
00:09:59,598 --> 00:10:01,017
-Well, he wasn't...
-He was not.

246
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,477
He was a modern version of that,
'cause he was...

247
00:10:03,561 --> 00:10:06,480
-Right.
-He was denying us, but in an extremely,

248
00:10:06,564 --> 00:10:09,734
"This is hurting me more
than it hurts you" way. It's...

249
00:10:10,526 --> 00:10:12,278
In the old days, you didn't say that.

250
00:10:12,361 --> 00:10:13,821
-Right.
-"What I just tell ya?!"

251
00:10:13,904 --> 00:10:15,573
You know? It was more like that.

252
00:10:15,656 --> 00:10:18,451
"I can't have you in this store."

253
00:10:20,161 --> 00:10:20,995
Keep going.

254
00:10:21,078 --> 00:10:22,955
[man] Ask your man
if he wants to switch keys.

255
00:10:23,039 --> 00:10:25,166
Okay. Do you want to switch keys, Jerry?

256
00:10:25,249 --> 00:10:26,709
People love that joke.

257
00:10:26,792 --> 00:10:29,128
-You've heard that one before?
-Oh, yes, I have.

258
00:10:29,211 --> 00:10:30,046
[Matthew laughs]

259
00:10:32,423 --> 00:10:35,051
I love when people answer the question,
"Do you know what I mean?"

260
00:10:35,134 --> 00:10:36,719
"Yes, I do know what you mean."

261
00:10:36,802 --> 00:10:37,970
[Matthew laughs]

262
00:10:38,054 --> 00:10:40,139
-"Certainly, I do."
-"Why, certainly."

263
00:10:40,222 --> 00:10:42,558
That's an old movie way
to answer things there.

264
00:10:42,641 --> 00:10:44,226
-Abbott and Costello. 
-Oh, yeah.

265
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:46,646
"Certainly she does. Go ask her!"

266
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:49,482
-Do you like Abbott and Costello?
-What do you think?

267
00:10:49,565 --> 00:10:51,442
I think you like Abbott and Costello.

268
00:10:52,568 --> 00:10:56,572
[Jerry] Know what I watched the other day?
The Producers, the original from 1967.

269
00:10:56,656 --> 00:10:59,742
-Yeah.
-The overhead swastika shot,

270
00:10:59,825 --> 00:11:01,327
is that in... in the movie?

271
00:11:01,410 --> 00:11:02,745
-Yeah.
-When you guys did it?

272
00:11:02,828 --> 00:11:05,456
Yes, it's also...
They did it when we did the play...

273
00:11:05,539 --> 00:11:07,666
-[Jerry] Oh.
-...at great expense.

274
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:09,043
-Right.
-There's a mirror...

275
00:11:09,126 --> 00:11:10,336
-Right.
-...in back.

276
00:11:10,419 --> 00:11:11,879
-Wow.
-And halfway through it,

277
00:11:11,962 --> 00:11:13,047
it, uh...

278
00:11:13,547 --> 00:11:15,299
-tilts forward...
-Right.

279
00:11:15,383 --> 00:11:17,134
...so that the audience sees the swastika.

280
00:11:17,218 --> 00:11:18,594
How insane is that?

281
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:22,056
The audience would go mad 
when they had opened in Chicago.

282
00:11:22,139 --> 00:11:23,849
-Really?
-'Cause they know that shot,

283
00:11:23,933 --> 00:11:25,142
and they want it, you know.

284
00:11:25,226 --> 00:11:26,394
And it's a play. They're like,

285
00:11:26,477 --> 00:11:28,562
-"Oh, they're not gonna get to do that."
-Right.

286
00:11:28,646 --> 00:11:31,023
And sure enough,
the whole mirror tilts forward.

287
00:11:31,399 --> 00:11:33,567
-[Jerry laughs]
-And they just went crazy.

288
00:11:33,651 --> 00:11:34,777
It was so exciting.

289
00:11:35,903 --> 00:11:38,197
And, you know, the studio
would not release the movie.

290
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:39,365
They hated it so much.

291
00:11:39,448 --> 00:11:41,450
And you watch it
and you can easily see that happen.

292
00:11:41,534 --> 00:11:44,203
I mean, when he goes to visit, uh,
Kenneth Mars,

293
00:11:44,286 --> 00:11:47,206
he's got a picture of Hitler on the wall.

294
00:11:47,289 --> 00:11:50,793
It's only a handful of years
after World War II.

295
00:11:50,876 --> 00:11:53,462
-I know.
-It's like a September 11th comedy today.

296
00:11:53,546 --> 00:11:55,840
-Yes.
-That's about the same amount of time.

297
00:11:56,257 --> 00:11:59,468
-That's so crazy.
-And, obviously, a far worse tragedy.

298
00:11:59,552 --> 00:12:01,178
-Right?
-Right. Yeah.

299
00:12:01,262 --> 00:12:04,390
So picture someone
making a funny movie today

300
00:12:04,723 --> 00:12:06,851
-about September 11th.
-I know.

301
00:12:06,934 --> 00:12:08,769
That's how crazy that was.

302
00:12:08,853 --> 00:12:10,104
[Matthew chuckles] Yeah.

303
00:12:11,522 --> 00:12:13,357
[Jerry] As long as we're going 
to Citi Field...

304
00:12:13,441 --> 00:12:15,317
-Yes.
-...tell your Ralph Kiner story.

305
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:16,861
Well, Ralph Kiner,

306
00:12:16,944 --> 00:12:18,279
a great player and announcer.

307
00:12:18,362 --> 00:12:19,697
-[Jerry] Yeah.
-[Matthew] You know?

308
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:22,158
He came backstage
when I was doing The Producers.

309
00:12:22,241 --> 00:12:24,368
-I was so excited to meet him.
-Wow.

310
00:12:24,452 --> 00:12:28,497
And I told him a heartwarming story
about... that my father,

311
00:12:29,206 --> 00:12:30,791
when I was little, would say, uh,

312
00:12:31,125 --> 00:12:34,378
"Oh, I don't like when, uh,
Bob Murphy takes over.

313
00:12:34,462 --> 00:12:35,838
I feel like then the Mets lose.

314
00:12:35,921 --> 00:12:38,299
I think they do better
when Kiner is talking."

315
00:12:38,382 --> 00:12:39,216
Yeah.

316
00:12:39,508 --> 00:12:40,342
So...

317
00:12:40,801 --> 00:12:42,887
I told... I got to tell Ralph Kiner that,

318
00:12:42,970 --> 00:12:43,888
and he said,

319
00:12:44,346 --> 00:12:48,684
"Well, we didn't really have very much
effect on the, uh, outcome of the games

320
00:12:49,059 --> 00:12:50,644
when we were calling them.

321
00:12:50,728 --> 00:12:52,354
Didn't have much effect on it."

322
00:12:53,355 --> 00:12:55,524
-"Oh, okay. Yeah, I guess...
-[Jerry laughs]

323
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:57,735
I guess that's true."

324
00:12:57,818 --> 00:12:58,819
[Jerry laughs]

325
00:12:58,903 --> 00:13:02,364
My father was, of course, a mental case...

326
00:13:02,448 --> 00:13:03,282
[Jerry laughs]

327
00:13:03,365 --> 00:13:04,950
And how stupid he thinks you are.

328
00:13:05,034 --> 00:13:08,245
How stupid do you think I am?
How stupid do you think my father was?

329
00:13:08,329 --> 00:13:09,622
[Jerry laughs]

330
00:13:09,705 --> 00:13:12,208
-Yeah.
-But that... what it tells you is,

331
00:13:12,416 --> 00:13:15,169
-the kind of people that come up to him.
-That's true.

332
00:13:15,252 --> 00:13:17,796
He just feels like he should
take everything seriously.

333
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:20,382
-Yeah. He's trying to be nice about it.
-Very nice.

334
00:13:20,633 --> 00:13:22,384
"No, I'm sorry to break it to you,

335
00:13:22,468 --> 00:13:26,430
our calling the game didn't have
much effect on the outcome." Yeah.

336
00:13:26,514 --> 00:13:28,307
["Meet The Mets" plays]

337
00:13:29,975 --> 00:13:31,435
♪ Meet The Mets ♪

338
00:13:32,019 --> 00:13:33,103
♪ Meet The Mets ♪

339
00:13:33,813 --> 00:13:35,814
Hey, you know what? This is a good idea.

340
00:13:36,065 --> 00:13:37,316
Of course, it... yeah.

341
00:13:37,399 --> 00:13:38,692
♪ Bring your kiddies ♪

342
00:13:38,776 --> 00:13:39,902
♪ Bring your wife ♪

343
00:13:40,319 --> 00:13:43,280
♪ Guaranteed to have
The time of your life ♪

344
00:13:43,364 --> 00:13:45,324
-There he is, Ralph Kiner.
-There he is.

345
00:13:45,866 --> 00:13:47,618
Now, if you're a Ralph Kiner,

346
00:13:47,701 --> 00:13:49,870
-A handsome fellow.
-Yeah.

347
00:13:49,954 --> 00:13:52,206
And then they make your face in bronze...

348
00:13:52,790 --> 00:13:55,334
for the Hall of Fame,
and they give you a double chin.

349
00:13:55,417 --> 00:13:57,837
-Why would they do that?
-Why would they do that?

350
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:00,339
These people that do this stuff,
they stink.

351
00:14:00,422 --> 00:14:02,466
And by the way,
they always make the teeth look like

352
00:14:02,550 --> 00:14:05,052
you just had
those chocolate wafer cookies.

353
00:14:05,427 --> 00:14:07,096
You know those ones that get all in there?

354
00:14:07,179 --> 00:14:08,013
[laughing] I do.

355
00:14:08,097 --> 00:14:09,890
I guess it's not a problem
we're gonna have.

356
00:14:09,974 --> 00:14:10,933
[Jerry laughs]

357
00:14:12,059 --> 00:14:13,978
-Now, just imagine...
-Yeah.

358
00:14:14,061 --> 00:14:16,063
-This is the fantasy, right?
-Right.

359
00:14:16,146 --> 00:14:17,356
-We've just--
-We've signed.

360
00:14:17,439 --> 00:14:18,941
-We've been signed.
-Yeah.

361
00:14:19,024 --> 00:14:20,901
And you're coming up from Pawtucket.

362
00:14:21,610 --> 00:14:23,904
-Yeah.
-And you're a major leaguer.

363
00:14:24,613 --> 00:14:26,740
"Jerry, what do you want to hit a ball?"

364
00:14:26,824 --> 00:14:28,284
No, ma. This is a real--

365
00:14:28,367 --> 00:14:31,745
-They're paying money for it now. Yeah.
-Ma, the great opportunity for our family.

366
00:14:31,829 --> 00:14:33,872
[Jerry] I think before we go out
on the field...

367
00:14:33,956 --> 00:14:37,001
-You're gonna do some lifting?
-...we should work out a little bit.

368
00:14:37,084 --> 00:14:38,210
[Matthew laughs]

369
00:14:38,294 --> 00:14:41,297
I think one set of one exercise
will be enough.

370
00:14:41,672 --> 00:14:43,465
-That should do it.
-[Jerry laughs]

371
00:14:43,549 --> 00:14:45,217
We don't want to get too big.

372
00:14:45,301 --> 00:14:46,886
-Yeah, you don't wanna bulk up.
-No.

373
00:14:46,969 --> 00:14:48,929
♪ Come on and meet the Mets ♪

374
00:14:49,346 --> 00:14:50,556
♪ Meet the Mets ♪

375
00:14:51,265 --> 00:14:53,726
That thing looks like it
probably measures your BMI.

376
00:14:53,809 --> 00:14:55,644
-Let's not do that.
-[Jerry laughs]

377
00:14:55,728 --> 00:14:56,896
I'm not likin'.

378
00:14:58,230 --> 00:15:00,274
I wonder if they work out here
over the winter.

379
00:15:00,357 --> 00:15:01,483
[candy wrapper crunches]

380
00:15:01,567 --> 00:15:02,484
Looks busy.

381
00:15:06,363 --> 00:15:07,823
All right, let's shower up.

382
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:10,159
-Isn't this fun?
-It is fun.

383
00:15:10,242 --> 00:15:11,577
I'm having a great time.

384
00:15:12,077 --> 00:15:14,246
-Here's our stuff.
-Oh, look at that.

385
00:15:15,164 --> 00:15:17,124
This is where
they have to answer those questions.

386
00:15:17,207 --> 00:15:20,336
Yeah, well, our back's up against it now,
but that's how it's been all year.

387
00:15:20,878 --> 00:15:25,007
And all you're trying to do is read
the different products over their head,

388
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:27,009
-that they have, right?
-[Matthew laughs]

389
00:15:27,092 --> 00:15:29,428
-Oh, look at that. He uses Ammens Powder.
-Yeah.

390
00:15:29,511 --> 00:15:31,055
[both laugh]

391
00:15:31,472 --> 00:15:32,640
[Jerry] Let's go, uh...

392
00:15:33,015 --> 00:15:34,099
show 'em what we got.

393
00:15:34,475 --> 00:15:35,893
["Joltin' Joe DiMaggio" plays]

394
00:15:43,108 --> 00:15:44,401
This is good.

395
00:15:44,485 --> 00:15:48,697
♪ He started baseball's famous streak
That's got us all aglow ♪

396
00:15:48,906 --> 00:15:50,991
♪ He's just a man and not a freak ♪

397
00:15:51,075 --> 00:15:51,909
[grunts]

398
00:15:51,992 --> 00:15:53,827
♪ Joltin' Joe DiMaggio ♪

399
00:15:55,329 --> 00:15:56,163
[grunts]

400
00:15:56,872 --> 00:15:57,998
Oh, come on!

401
00:15:59,041 --> 00:16:00,167
Come on!

402
00:16:04,171 --> 00:16:05,798
[cheering]

403
00:16:06,590 --> 00:16:07,424
[cheering]

404
00:16:07,508 --> 00:16:08,509
Go!

405
00:16:08,592 --> 00:16:10,511
♪ Joe, Joe DiMaggio ♪

406
00:16:11,387 --> 00:16:12,304
Cheers.

407
00:16:13,472 --> 00:16:14,515
-[Jerry] Oh, yeah.
-[grunts]

408
00:16:25,859 --> 00:16:26,777
[laughs]

409
00:16:28,612 --> 00:16:30,322
[upbeat music plays]

410
00:16:31,991 --> 00:16:34,034
[Jerry] After we asked for this car,

411
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:37,246
and before they gave it to us,
somebody bought it.

412
00:16:37,871 --> 00:16:38,956
-Really?
-Yeah.

413
00:16:39,289 --> 00:16:41,125
When shooting Ferris Bueller,

414
00:16:41,208 --> 00:16:43,127
I remember a few days before,

415
00:16:43,544 --> 00:16:45,587
they were having some trouble
with a Ferrari,

416
00:16:45,671 --> 00:16:48,132
and a car kind of like this came.

417
00:16:48,549 --> 00:16:49,717
-Really?
-Yes.

418
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:52,428
And the director was trying to decide,
he was asking everybody,

419
00:16:52,511 --> 00:16:55,597
"Should I do the classic one
or the super modern..."

420
00:16:56,223 --> 00:16:59,309
-Well, he made the right decision there.
-I think he did.

421
00:16:59,393 --> 00:17:01,687
We had two fake ones that I drove,

422
00:17:01,770 --> 00:17:04,398
and there was another one to just
throw out the window, basically.

423
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:07,943
And here's what's interesting also,
that movie,

424
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:11,780
the other... obviously, the big theme,
was baseball. 

425
00:17:11,864 --> 00:17:13,615
-Wrigley Field.
-Yeah.

426
00:17:13,699 --> 00:17:15,659
So, there's quite a similarity

427
00:17:15,743 --> 00:17:17,953
-to, uh, the show you're making today.
-Yep.

428
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:19,538
[upbeat music plays]

429
00:17:21,165 --> 00:17:23,751
[Matthew] A driver once told me
Woody Allen won't go in the tunnels,

430
00:17:23,834 --> 00:17:25,544
you have to go over the bridge.

431
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:27,755
He's too scared of the tunnels.

432
00:17:29,006 --> 00:17:31,467
That's a good, uh, therapist he's got.

433
00:17:31,550 --> 00:17:32,384
[Matthew laughs]

434
00:17:32,468 --> 00:17:34,887
-Made a lot of good progress.
-[Matthew laughs]

435
00:17:38,724 --> 00:17:40,976
Tell me when the light turns.

436
00:17:41,435 --> 00:17:44,146
You can keep doing that while you drive.
I think that's fine.

437
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:47,816
[Matthew] God, I just... I have... 

438
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:50,319
I'm tempted to open this door 
and have it get ripped off.

439
00:17:50,402 --> 00:17:51,904
Have another car just rip it right off.

440
00:17:51,987 --> 00:17:54,364
-That'd be hilarious.
-I don't know why I wanna do that.

441
00:17:54,448 --> 00:17:57,076
The next scene would be
the guy who bought the car,

442
00:17:57,326 --> 00:17:58,786
us talking to him.

443
00:17:58,869 --> 00:17:59,912
I just want to explain---

444
00:17:59,995 --> 00:18:01,747
Take it easy. You're
gonna notice a little...

445
00:18:01,830 --> 00:18:03,540
-Just take it easy.
-Yeah.

446
00:18:03,624 --> 00:18:04,625
[both laugh]

447
00:18:05,334 --> 00:18:06,627
[instrumental music plays]

448
00:18:18,806 --> 00:18:19,973
Oh, we're really just eating?

449
00:18:20,432 --> 00:18:22,726
-Yeah.
-We're not saying anything entertaining.

450
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:25,646
It's like therapy.

451
00:18:26,355 --> 00:18:28,190
I don't know anything about therapy.

452
00:18:28,690 --> 00:18:31,860
I don't really like to talk about things
that are bothering me.

453
00:18:32,444 --> 00:18:33,362
I don't either.

454
00:18:34,154 --> 00:18:35,697
When you go there, you enjoy it.

455
00:18:36,406 --> 00:18:37,783
I don't always enjoy it.

456
00:18:38,367 --> 00:18:40,202
Well, nobody always enjoys anything.

457
00:18:41,578 --> 00:18:42,746
I think we're fighting.

458
00:18:42,830 --> 00:18:43,872
[both laugh]

459
00:18:45,624 --> 00:18:50,504
I would not be interested
in any kind of psychiatrist or therapist

460
00:18:51,004 --> 00:18:52,422
under 70.

461
00:18:53,048 --> 00:18:54,466
You want a sort of father.

462
00:18:55,634 --> 00:18:56,468
Oh.

463
00:18:57,845 --> 00:19:00,722
Well, I think I just cracked a nut there.
I just...

464
00:19:02,140 --> 00:19:05,018
But, yes. You want your father's advice.

465
00:19:05,435 --> 00:19:07,563
Yeah. Or your mom, you know?

466
00:19:08,772 --> 00:19:09,606
Yeah.

467
00:19:10,399 --> 00:19:11,733
A calm mom.

468
00:19:11,817 --> 00:19:12,693
A calm... [laughs]

469
00:19:12,776 --> 00:19:15,946
-Not the one that's yelling all the time.
-I think I just cracked a nut.

470
00:19:16,029 --> 00:19:17,406
[both laugh]

471
00:19:18,574 --> 00:19:21,702
[Matthew] Yeah, sometimes I'll hear
a little bit of another patient.

472
00:19:22,452 --> 00:19:24,997
'Cause he's slow to his answering thing.
And so...

473
00:19:25,789 --> 00:19:27,958
I'll hear, "Hello, Doctor. I...

474
00:19:28,709 --> 00:19:29,751
I can't make Tuesday."

475
00:19:30,335 --> 00:19:33,505
Then he reaches for the thing,
you hear some whining.

476
00:19:34,298 --> 00:19:36,884
"I talked to Nicole,
and there's just no way we--"

477
00:19:42,931 --> 00:19:44,766
Ah. That was good.

478
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:46,852
-Damn, that's good coffee.
-Yeah, good coffee.

479
00:20:00,616 --> 00:20:01,992
Tammy, do we have hot dogs?

480
00:20:02,659 --> 00:20:03,660
Yay!

